※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ In a generic body, `⎕drop` of a value whose type is the one a type parameter
※ stands for takes off whatever unit that type carries -- none included, which
※ is the value as it is.  Whether there is one is what the call said, and the
※ body cannot know; refusing it there (4543) would leave a generic function no
※ way to write a number with a unit and one without in one definition.  A unit
※ says nothing about the bits, so the two instances do the same thing.

let std := ⎕import("std")

unit ¤meter → ¤idx

※ In an arm asking what kind `T'` is, as anything done to a value of a type
※ parameter without a requirement saying so is: the arm is checked for the
※ types a call gives it, and not where it is written.
fn doubled(v: T') → u64:
    comptime if ⎕kindof(⌜T'⌝) = "integer":
        ⎕widen(⎕drop(v), ⌜u64⌝) × 2u64
    else:
        0u64

@[startup]
fn main(init: std.Init) → u6:
    let far: u32 ¤meter = 21
    if doubled(far) ≠ 42u64:
        return 1u6
    if doubled(5u32) ≠ 10u64:
        return 2u6
    let s: u8⟦⟧ = ⎕bytes("abc")
    if doubled(#s) ≠ 6u64:
        return 3u6
    0u6
